Concrete issues:

1. `incomplete_integration_with_existing_code` — severity 5, confidence 0.99  
   Location: `src/utils/standard-site-paths.ts:54`  
   Code:
   ```ts
   export function standardSitePublicationPath(
     did: string,
     publicationRkey: string,
   ): string {
     requireRouteParts(did, publicationRkey);
     return `/at/${encodeURIComponent(did)}/${publicationRkey}`;
   }
   ```
   The new module has no production callsite; repository search found it imported only by its new test. The actual publisher remains `src/utils/standard-site.ts`: it still stores `window.location.origin` as the publication URL, writes document paths as `/p/${folio.id}`, derives the rkey with unchecked `uri.split("/").pop()`, and uses the old cached-record lifecycle. Consequently the new canonical paths and validators do not affect publishing, lifecycle, or runtime behavior at all.

2. `commit_message_mismatch` — severity 5, confidence 0.99  
   Location: `src/utils/standard-site-paths.ts:75`  
   Code:
   ```ts
   export function standardSiteDocumentUrl(
     did: string,
     publicationRkey: string,
     documentRkey: string,
     origin = STANDARD_SITE_ORIGIN,
   ): string {
     return `${standardSitePublicationUrl(did, publicationRkey, origin)}${standardSiteDocumentPath(documentRkey)}`;
   }

   export function standardSiteVerificationPath(
     did: string,
     publicationRkey: string,
   ): string {
     return `/.well-known/site.standard.publication${standardSitePublicationPath(did, publicationRkey)}`;
   }
   ```
   These functions only manufacture URL strings. The diff adds no Qwik route for `/at/<did>/<publication-rkey>` or its document child, no PDS lookup/rendering reader, no handler at the generated `.well-known` path that returns the publication AT-URI, and no document `<link rel="site.standard.document">`. Thus every new canonical URL/verification path lacks an implementation and will resolve through the existing router as 404. Standard.site explicitly requires a serving endpoint and document link, not merely path construction ([verification specification](https://standard.site/docs/verification/)).

No separate issues found for:

- `documentation_implementation_mismatch` — the misleading behavioral implication in the module comment is the same missing-route defect covered by issue 2, so reporting it again would duplicate the issue.
- `instruction_file_disobeyed`
- `user_request_artifacts_left_in_code`
- `repetitive_or_duplicate_code` — the duplicated collection literals are trivial compared with, and subsumed by, the missing integration.
- `abstraction_violation`

